From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meenmachine1 <78238681+meenmachine1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:10:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Decompiler: an option to type a loaded value by its
 instruction's extension

- Shows: `ldrh r1,[r0]; add r1,r1,#1; strh r1,[r0]` (an unsigned short
  incremented in place) decompiles as `short *param_1; *param_1 =
  *param_1 + 1;`, and `ldrh r0,[r0]; bx lr` returns `undefined2`. The
  instruction says the value is unsigned; the C says signed or nothing, so
  C written from it (a matching decompilation) compiles to `ldrsh` or
  needs the type guessed.
- Cause: the zero-extension the instruction applies (INT_ZEXT of the
  LOAD's output) is simplified away before types are inferred (the
  SUBPIECE of the ZEXT'd sum is the 2-byte sum), so the loaded value is
  typed by its remaining uses alone, here an INT_ADD (signed).
- Patch: a new option, `extensiontypes` (off by default, so nothing
  changes unless it is set). With it on, FlowInfo marks each LOAD whose
  output its own instruction reads with INT_SEXT as sign-extending, and
  with INT_ZEXT, for a value smaller than an int, as zero-extending (an
  int-sized zero-extension is how x86-64 and AArch64 write a 32-bit
  result, not a choice of signedness). TypeOpLoad::getOutputLocal then
  gives the output a signed or unsigned integer type instead of unknown,
  which type propagation weighs with the uses as before: an unsigned use
  of a sign-extended value still wins (SuperH's mov.b followed by
  extu.b stays unsigned).
  `extensiontypes words` leaves out zero-extended bytes: compilers load a
  signed char with movzx or ldrb where only its low bits matter, and the
  unsigned type would displace the char a string or buffer has.
- Seen on: ARMv5TE (Nintendo DS, CodeWarrior) code in a matching
  decompilation; the same on MIPS (lhu), PowerPC (lhz), x86 (movzx).
- Ghidra 12.1.4.

